### StyloBot Trailblazor demo — HTTP probes
###
### Run with:  dotnet run --project Mostlylucid.BotDetection.TrailblazorDemo
### Then run any probe below from JetBrains Rider, VS Code REST Client,
### or httpyac / kulala.nvim.
###
### IMPORTANT: human probes send the full Sec-Ch-Ua / Sec-Fetch-* headers
### that real Chrome 120 always emits. Sending the Chrome UA string WITHOUT
### them trips the Inconsistency detector (correctly — bots commonly forge
### the UA but forget client hints). That's a teaching moment for the talk:
### the engine isn't fooled by the UA alone.

@host = http://localhost:5186
@bypassKey = demo-bypass-key-localhost-only

@humanUa  = Mozilla/5.0 (Windows NT 10.0; Win64; x64) AppleWebKit/537.36 (KHTML, like Gecko) Chrome/120.0.0.0 Safari/537.36
@google   = Mozilla/5.0 (compatible; Googlebot/2.1; +http://www.google.com/bot.html)
@scrapy   = Scrapy/2.5.0 (+https://scrapy.org)
@masscan  = masscan/1.0 (https://github.com/robertdavidgraham/masscan)


### --- 1. The home page — server-rendered Razor with TagHelpers ----------

### Plain human visitor (full browser-shape headers)
GET {{host}}/
User-Agent: {{humanUa}}
Accept: text/html,application/xhtml+xml,application/xml;q=0.9,image/avif,image/webp,*/*;q=0.8
Accept-Language: en-US,en;q=0.9
Accept-Encoding: gzip, deflate, br
Sec-Ch-Ua: "Chromium";v="120", "Not(A:Brand";v="24", "Google Chrome";v="120"
Sec-Ch-Ua-Mobile: ?0
Sec-Ch-Ua-Platform: "Windows"
Sec-Fetch-Mode: navigate
Sec-Fetch-Dest: document
Sec-Fetch-Site: none
Upgrade-Insecure-Requests: 1


### Flip the engine's view of you to googlebot via query-param test mode
### (TestModeQueryParam = "demo" in appsettings).
GET {{host}}/?demo=googlebot
User-Agent: {{humanUa}}
Accept: text/html


### Flip to scrapy
GET {{host}}/?demo=scrapy
User-Agent: {{humanUa}}
Accept: text/html


### Flip to masscan
GET {{host}}/?demo=malicious
User-Agent: {{humanUa}}
Accept: text/html


### --- 2. Diagnostic endpoint — full per-detector breakdown ---------------

### Human-UA full check (with realistic chrome headers)
GET {{host}}/bot-detection/check
User-Agent: {{humanUa}}
Accept: application/json
Accept-Language: en-US,en;q=0.9
Sec-Ch-Ua: "Chromium";v="120", "Not(A:Brand";v="24", "Google Chrome";v="120"
Sec-Ch-Ua-Mobile: ?0
Sec-Ch-Ua-Platform: "Windows"


### Direct bot-UA check (no test mode — the real UA + minimal headers, as a
### scraper would actually send)
GET {{host}}/bot-detection/check
User-Agent: {{scrapy}}


### Stats — reflects DemoPreloadOnStartup + every real request the
### middleware (or BlockBots filter on-demand) has run detection on.
GET {{host}}/bot-detection/stats


### Health
GET {{host}}/bot-detection/health


### --- 3. Traffic shaping — .BlockBots() and .BotPolicy() ----------------

### /api/data is .BlockBots() — humans (with chrome client hints) pass.
GET {{host}}/api/data
User-Agent: {{humanUa}}
Accept: application/json
Accept-Language: en-US,en;q=0.9
Sec-Ch-Ua: "Chromium";v="120", "Not(A:Brand";v="24", "Google Chrome";v="120"
Sec-Ch-Ua-Mobile: ?0
Sec-Ch-Ua-Platform: "Windows"
Sec-Fetch-Mode: cors
Sec-Fetch-Dest: empty
Sec-Fetch-Site: same-origin
Referer: {{host}}/


### /api/data with a scraper UA — 403, botType=Scraper
GET {{host}}/api/data
User-Agent: {{scrapy}}


### /api/data with masscan UA — 403, MaliciousBot
GET {{host}}/api/data
User-Agent: {{masscan}}


### /api/data with the dev bypass key — passes regardless of UA.
### Demonstrates the operator-trust escape hatch (CI, monitoring, etc.)
GET {{host}}/api/data
User-Agent: {{scrapy}}
X-SB-Api-Key: {{bypassKey}}


### /sitemap.xml is .BlockBots(allowVerifiedBots: true, allowSearchEngines: true).
### Googlebot UA — passes
GET {{host}}/sitemap.xml
User-Agent: {{google}}


### /sitemap.xml with scrapy — refused (search-engine whitelist doesn't
### cover Scraper bot type). NOTE: BlockBots now re-runs detection on
### demand when the middleware short-circuited via the static-asset
### policy (.xml extensions hit it by default).
GET {{host}}/sitemap.xml
User-Agent: {{scrapy}}


### /sitemap.xml with chrome — also refused, because human browsers
### don't directly fetch /sitemap.xml. If you see chrome here, it's a
### chrome-shaped bot. Teaching moment for the talk.
GET {{host}}/sitemap.xml
User-Agent: {{humanUa}}


### /api/login has .BotPolicy("strict", blockThreshold: 0.5) — humans
### with realistic browser headers pass.
POST {{host}}/api/login
Content-Type: application/json
User-Agent: {{humanUa}}
Accept: application/json
Accept-Language: en-US,en;q=0.9
Sec-Ch-Ua: "Chromium";v="120", "Not(A:Brand";v="24", "Google Chrome";v="120"
Sec-Ch-Ua-Mobile: ?0
Sec-Ch-Ua-Platform: "Windows"
Sec-Fetch-Mode: cors
Sec-Fetch-Dest: empty
Sec-Fetch-Site: same-origin
Origin: {{host}}
Referer: {{host}}/

{ "user": "scott", "password": "hunter2" }


### /api/login with scrapy — refused by the strict policy
POST {{host}}/api/login
Content-Type: application/json
User-Agent: {{scrapy}}

{ "user": "scott", "password": "hunter2" }


### --- 4. Honeypot ------------------------------------------------------

### Clean form submit — succeeds normally (real subscribe path)
POST {{host}}/Home/Subscribe
Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded
User-Agent: {{humanUa}}

email=demo@example.com


### Bot-style submit — fills the honeypot trap field (sb_email_confirm).
### Controller silently redirects to /Subscribed without performing the
### work. Use the bypass key so we're testing the honeypot, not the
### detector.
POST {{host}}/Home/Subscribe
Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded
User-Agent: {{humanUa}}
X-SB-Api-Key: {{bypassKey}}

email=demo@example.com&sb_email_confirm=trap@bot.invalid


### --- 5. Dashboard --------------------------------------------------------

### Dashboard landing (HTML). Open in a browser for the live feed.
GET {{host}}/_stylobot/
User-Agent: {{humanUa}}